1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,364
Every March, satellites light up with fire detections over Bangladesh.

2
00:00:05,364 --> 00:00:10,737
Two NASA satellites watch the same fires. The older one sees one hot pixel.

3
00:00:10,737 --> 00:00:14,892
The sharper one sees several. So which count do you trust?

4
00:00:15,792 --> 00:00:17,935
We are Team Lumos, from Dhaka.

5
00:00:17,935 --> 00:00:22,365
We built Parallax Fire for the NASA Space Apps Challenge 2026,

6
00:00:22,365 --> 00:00:28,653
to turn two satellite records that disagree into one honest story of when a place burns.

7
00:00:29,553 --> 00:00:35,346
Here is the problem. MODIS has recorded fires for more than twenty years,

8
00:00:35,346 --> 00:00:41,536
in pixels one kilometre wide. VIIRS arrived in 2012 with pixels of 375 metres,

9
00:00:41,536 --> 00:00:43,758
so it catches smaller fires.

10
00:00:43,758 --> 00:00:48,599
In Bangladesh it records about five times as many detections.

11
00:00:48,599 --> 00:00:53,996
Join the two records, and burning seems to jump in 2012. But mostly,

12
00:00:53,996 --> 00:00:55,504
the camera changed.

13
00:00:56,404 --> 00:01:00,492
Imagine you plan for the burning season in your district.

14
00:01:00,492 --> 00:01:04,222
You compare 2005 with 2020, see five times the fire,

15
00:01:04,222 --> 00:01:06,876
and ask for five times the resources,

16
00:01:06,876 --> 00:01:10,247
for a change that never happened on the ground.

17
00:01:10,247 --> 00:01:14,837
And a hot spot is not always a wildfire. It can be crop burning,

18
00:01:14,837 --> 00:01:18,136
or one fire seen twice. Cloud hides fires too,

19
00:01:18,136 --> 00:01:20,718
so no detection never means no fire.

20
00:01:21,618 --> 00:01:26,563
So here is how you use Parallax Fire. Open the calendar and pick your place.

21
00:01:26,563 --> 00:01:29,165
Each row is a year, each column a month,

22
00:01:29,165 --> 00:01:34,175
and the colour shows how much burning the satellites recorded. In Bangladesh,

23
00:01:34,175 --> 00:01:38,599
March and April hold about four in five detections, year after year.

24
00:01:38,599 --> 00:01:42,177
Choose California, and the season moves to late summer.

25
00:01:43,077 --> 00:01:47,145
Pick a month, and the map shows where it burned, on a real map,

26
00:01:47,145 --> 00:01:49,727
by five kilometre square or by district.

27
00:01:49,727 --> 00:01:54,441
Heat view and night vision change only how the map looks, never the data.

28
00:01:55,341 --> 00:01:59,101
Open Two satellites to see both records side by side,

29
00:01:59,101 --> 00:02:05,344
with our estimate of what the sharper satellite would have counted in the earlier years.

30
00:02:05,344 --> 00:02:09,743
The estimate learns only from years when both satellites flew,

31
00:02:09,743 --> 00:02:13,1000
and we checked it on 2023 and 2024, years it had never seen.

32
00:02:13,1000 --> 00:02:18,257
In Bangladesh it missed by 98 detections a month on average.

33
00:02:18,257 --> 00:02:22,230
Taking the old satellite’s count as it is misses by 627.

34
00:02:23,130 --> 00:02:25,226
We also show where it falls short.

35
00:02:25,226 --> 00:02:29,233
In New South Wales the estimate was closer than the simple rules,

36
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:32,438
but its likely range was too wide to pass our check,

37
00:02:32,438 --> 00:02:37,123
so there the app shows the raw comparison only. Missing months stay missing.

38
00:02:37,123 --> 00:02:39,712
Every estimate is labelled as an estimate.

39
00:02:40,612 --> 00:02:41,467
And for today,

40
00:02:41,467 --> 00:02:46,778
the Live page shows the last twenty-four hours of NASA fire detections on a real globe,

41
00:02:46,778 --> 00:02:48,120
counted per satellite,

42
00:02:48,120 --> 00:02:53,004
with fresh satellite pictures and the position of each fire satellite right now.

43
00:02:53,904 --> 00:02:55,250
What does this change?

44
00:02:55,250 --> 00:03:00,453
A planner can see the real burning season before deciding when to watch more closely.

45
00:03:00,453 --> 00:03:05,533
A researcher can compare two decades without mistaking a new camera for a new fire.

46
00:03:05,533 --> 00:03:08,470
A teacher can show students why data needs care.

47
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:12,143
And every number can be traced to its source and downloaded.

48
00:03:13,043 --> 00:03:15,318
Next, we want to add more regions,

49
00:03:15,318 --> 00:03:19,803
follow the newer NOAA satellites as the Suomi NPP data stream ends,

50
00:03:19,803 --> 00:03:21,343
add a Bangla interface,

51
00:03:21,343 --> 00:03:25,828
and test it with the people who plan for burning seasons. For that,

52
00:03:25,828 --> 00:03:28,238
we need partners and field feedback.

53
00:03:29,138 --> 00:03:35,174
Parallax Fire. Same fires, two cameras, one honest record. Thank you.
